' nomo 1
' # Complex numbers
' The imaginary unit is `i`, an ordinary constant. Juxtaposition is already
' multiplication, so `4i` is `4*i` and needs no special number syntax — the
' same reading that makes `2e` mean `2*e`.

z = 3 + 4i
w = 1 - 2i

' Arithmetic. Any operation with a complex operand on either side answers
' complex, promoting the real one.
sum = z + w
difference = z - w
product = z*w
quotient = z/w
scaled = 2*z
negated = 0 - z

' `i` squared is where the whole thing comes from.
i^2

' Taking a value apart. `Re` and `Im` give real numbers; `Im` is the
' coefficient of `i`, so `Re(z) + Im(z)*i` rebuilds `z`.
Re(z)
Im(z)
conj(z)
abs(z)
arg(z)

' Whole powers only, by repeated multiplication. A fractional power of a
' complex number needs a branch cut, which this language does not choose.
z^2
z^3
z^0
z^(0 - 1)

' A complex value stays complex. Nothing demotes when the imaginary part
' happens to be zero, so a result's type never depends on its value.
real_again = z - 4i

' Units. Both parts share one dimension, because they are components of one
' measurement rather than two measurements.
Z = (3 + 4i) Ω
Z -> kΩ
abs(Z)
Re(Z)
' The argument is an angle, so it is dimensionless whatever Z is measured in.
arg(Z)

' The impedance of a series RLC branch at one frequency, which is what the
' corpus uses complex arithmetic for.
R = 32.3383 Ω
L = 4.58533e-5 H
C = 4.56546e-8 F
f = 110000 Hz
Zrlc = R + 2*pi*f*L*i + 1/(2*pi*f*C*i)
abs(Zrlc) -> Ω
arg(Zrlc)*180/pi
